# minimum required Qore module
%requires qore >= 3.0

%modern

%requires(reexport) Util
%requires Logger
%requires(reexport) HttpServerUtil

module AsyncSocketIo {
    version = "1.0";
    desc    = "API for async socket I/O";
    author  = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url     = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage AsyncSocketIo Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section asyncsocketiointro AsyncSocketIo Module Introduction

    The %AsyncSocketIo module provides async socket I/O using the native
    @ref Qore::AsyncIoController controller and the operating system's event loop
    (epoll on Linux, kqueue on macOS/BSD).

    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController "AsyncSocketIoController" submits operations,
      cancels them by key or owner, schedules timers, and dispatches callbacks to worker threads.
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::AbstractStreamContext "AbstractStreamContext" gives protocols a common
      stream interface, implemented for HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 with backpressure and controller wakeups.
      Its @ref AsyncSocketIo::AbstractStreamContext::trySendData() "trySendData()" method returns
      @ref False when the send buffer is full, allowing callers to drop a slow consumer without blocking.
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::AsyncHttp2ServerStream "AsyncHttp2ServerStream" sends HTTP/2 headers,
      data, and trailers through the async controller, including gRPC responses.
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::NotifierPollOperation "NotifierPollOperation" waits for event notifiers
      through the I/O controller instead of polling sockets on handler threads.

    Timers can carry an owner identifier. Before destroying their owner, cancel outstanding timers
    and drain dispatched callbacks with
    @ref AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController::flushCallbacksByOwner() "flushCallbacksByOwner()".

    To use this module, use \c "%requires AsyncSocketIo" in your code.

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the AsyncSocketIo namespace.

    @section asyncsocketioconfig AsyncSocketIo Configuration

    @par QORE_IO_THREADS Environment Variable
    The \c QORE_IO_THREADS environment variable controls the number of I/O threads used by the
    async I/O controller. I/O threads are CPU-bound (epoll/kqueue + continuePoll), so more threads
    than CPUs adds context-switch overhead without benefit. The default is auto-detected from
    \c hardware_concurrency() (the number of logical CPUs). Set this environment variable to any
    positive integer to override the default; for example:
    @code{.py}
    export QORE_IO_THREADS=4
    @endcode

    @section asyncsocketiorelnotes Release Notes

    @subsection asyncsocketio_1_0 AsyncSocketIo 1.0
    - initial release

    @section asyncsocketiointernals Internal Design

    This module provides a thin wrapper around the native C++ @ref Qore::AsyncIoController class,
    which implements a high-performance event loop for managing multiple concurrent socket operations.
    The C++ controller uses the most efficient I/O mechanism available on each platform (epoll on Linux,
    kqueue on macOS/BSD) via the @ref Qore::EventLoop class.

    The controller runs a dedicated I/O thread that processes socket events using a three-phase polling cycle:
    - Phase 1 (Snapshot under lock): collect operations needing continuePoll()
    - Phase 2 (continuePoll outside lock): drive operations, collect results
    - Phase 3 (Update under lock + deferred delivery): update cache/EventLoop, deliver callbacks

    This module adds backward-compatible wrapper methods and the global controller singleton on top
    of the C++ implementation.
*/

#! The AsyncSocketIo namespace
/** All public declarations in the %AsyncSocketIo module are defined in this namespace
*/
public namespace AsyncSocketIo {
}
